/* Bassiet book, Depth 15: The Drowned Mind. Original text. DSM-5-TR terms. */
window.BOOK=window.BOOK||{};
BOOK['c15-mood']={depth:15,title:'The Grey Weather',
epi:'Moods drift in here like weather off a cold sea. This front came in and never moved on.',
c:['mood-disorders','catecholamines','toxicology'],
S:[
{h:'Depression',p:[
'**Major depressive disorder** (DSM-5-TR): at least **5 of 9** symptoms, present most of the day nearly every day for at least **2 weeks**, a clear change from the person\'s usual self, with real distress or loss of function. Think of two gates and seven rooms. At least one of the five must come through a gate: **depressed mood** or **loss of interest or pleasure (anhedonia)**. The seven rooms are sleep change (insomnia or hypersomnia), appetite or weight change, low energy, guilt or worthlessness, poor concentration or indecision, psychomotor slowing or agitation that others can see, and recurrent thoughts of death or suicide. A substance (alcohol, steroids, interferon) or a medical cause (hypothyroidism, Cushing, pancreatic cancer) must be excluded, and so must any past manic or hypomanic episode: a single manic episode turns the diagnosis into bipolar I.',
'Specifiers sharpen the picture. **Melancholic** features: near-total anhedonia, early-morning waking, worse in the morning, marked weight loss and guilt. **Atypical** features: mood still brightens with good news, plus hypersomnia, overeating, leaden heaviness of the limbs and long-standing sensitivity to rejection (historically the best MAOI responders). Also psychotic features (usually mood-congruent delusions of guilt, poverty or illness), seasonal pattern (winter; bright-light therapy), mixed features and anxious distress. **Persistent depressive disorder** (dysthymia): depressed mood on most days for at least **2 years** (1 year in children and adolescents), never free of it for more than 2 months. **Premenstrual dysphoric disorder**: mood lability, irritability and low mood in the luteal phase that lift within days of menstruation; SSRIs, even taken only in the luteal phase, help.',
'**Around childbirth** the weather has three grades. Postpartum **"blues"**: very common, mild tearfulness and lability peaking around day 3–5, gone within 2 weeks; reassure and follow up. **Depression with peripartum onset**: a major depressive episode beginning in pregnancy or within 4 weeks of delivery (in practice clinicians watch the whole first year); psychotherapy and SSRIs (sertraline is compatible with breastfeeding), or the GABA-A positive modulators **brexanolone** (IV infusion with monitoring for sedation) and **zuranolone** (oral, 14-day course; no driving for 12 hours after a dose). **Postpartum psychosis**: rare (about 1–2 per 1000), usually within the first 2 weeks, confusion, delusions about the baby, high risk of infanticide and suicide; an emergency needing admission, and often the first sign of bipolar disorder.',
'Biology: reduced monoamine signalling explains why the drugs work but not why they take weeks; the delay tracks slow adaptations (presynaptic 5-HT₁A autoreceptor desensitisation, rising BDNF, hippocampal neurogenesis). The HPA axis runs hot (high cortisol, failure to suppress on dexamethasone). Sleep studies show **shorter REM latency**, more REM early in the night and less slow-wave sleep. Always ask about **suicide**: the strongest predictor is a previous attempt; risk rises with a plan and access to means (firearms, stockpiled drugs), male sex, older age, living alone, substance use, chronic pain or illness and hopelessness. Women attempt more often; men die more often. Energy can return before mood in the first weeks of treatment, so that window needs the closest watch.'],
table:{cols:['Drug class','Examples and mechanism','Key adverse effects and rules'],rows:[
['SSRIs','fluoxetine, sertraline, citalopram, escitalopram, paroxetine; block the serotonin transporter','first line; 4–6 weeks to work; GI upset, sexual dysfunction, hyponatraemia (SIADH, elderly), bleeding with NSAIDs, QT (citalopram); suicidal thinking warning under 25; discontinuation syndrome (paroxetine, short half-life)'],
['SNRIs','venlafaxine, desvenlafaxine, duloxetine; block serotonin and noradrenaline transporters','raised blood pressure (venlafaxine); duloxetine also for neuropathic pain and fibromyalgia'],
['Multimodal','vortioxetine, vilazodone; SERT block plus 5-HT receptor actions','nausea; less sexual dysfunction (vortioxetine)'],
['TCAs','amitriptyline, nortriptyline, imipramine, clomipramine; block NE and 5-HT reuptake','anticholinergic, α₁ block (orthostasis), antihistamine (sedation); overdose: wide QRS, arrhythmia, coma, seizures; give sodium bicarbonate'],
['MAOIs','phenelzine, tranylcypromine, isocarboxazid, selegiline patch; block monoamine breakdown','hypertensive crisis with tyramine (aged cheese, cured meat, tap beer) or sympathomimetics; serotonin syndrome with SSRIs, pethidine, tramadol; wait 2 weeks after stopping an SSRI (5 weeks after fluoxetine)'],
['Atypical','bupropion (NE and DA reuptake), mirtazapine (α₂ antagonist), trazodone (5-HT₂ antagonist)','bupropion: no sexual dysfunction, helps smoking cessation, lowers seizure threshold (avoid in bulimia, anorexia, seizure disorders); mirtazapine: weight gain, sedation; trazodone: sedation, orthostasis, priapism'],
['NMDA antagonists','esketamine (nasal, with an oral antidepressant); dextromethorphan-bupropion (bupropion blocks CYP2D6 and keeps dextromethorphan levels up)','esketamine: treatment-resistant depression and acute suicidal ideation, acts within hours; dissociation, sedation, rise in blood pressure; given in clinic with 2 hours of observation'],
['GABA-A neurosteroids','brexanolone (IV), zuranolone (oral)','peripartum depression; sedation and loss of consciousness (brexanolone), driving impairment (zuranolone)']]},
key:'Five of nine, for two weeks, and one of the five must be low mood or lost pleasure. Before the first antidepressant, ask about past mania.',
s:[['pharm','**Serotonin syndrome**: within hours of adding or combining serotonergic drugs (SSRIs, SNRIs, MAOIs, linezolid, tramadol, pethidine, fentanyl, triptans, St John\'s wort, MDMA): agitation, hyperthermia, sweating, diarrhoea, tremor, and the key sign, **clonus and hyperreflexia** (legs more than arms). Stop the drugs, cool, benzodiazepines, then **cyproheptadine** (5-HT₂A antagonist). Contrast **neuroleptic malignant syndrome**: days, after a dopamine blocker, lead-pipe rigidity with slow reflexes and very high CK.'],['phys','**Electroconvulsive therapy** induces a brief generalised seizure under general anaesthesia and muscle relaxation. It is the fastest and most effective treatment for severe depression with psychosis, catatonia, refusal to eat or drink, or high suicide risk, and is safe in pregnancy and old age. Side effects: headache and temporary memory loss (anterograde and some retrograde, mostly for the treatment period). There is no absolute contraindication; raised intracranial pressure and a recent MI or stroke carry the highest risk.']]},
{h:'Bipolar disorder',p:[
'**Mania**: at least **1 week** (any length if admission is needed) of abnormally elevated, expansive or irritable mood **and** persistently raised energy or activity, most of the day nearly every day, with at least **3** further features (**4** if the mood is only irritable). Picture a lamp turned past its rating: it shines on itself (grandiosity), needs no dark (less need for sleep), hums without pause (pressured speech), jumps (flight of ideas, racing thoughts), flickers at every moth (distractibility), works all night (more goal-directed activity, agitation) and finally burns the house (risky spending, sex, driving, investments). Marked impairment, admission or psychotic features make it mania. **Hypomania**: at least **4 days**, an observable change, but no marked impairment, no psychosis and no admission.',
'**Bipolar I**: at least one manic episode (depression is common but not required). **Bipolar II**: at least one hypomanic episode and at least one major depressive episode, never mania; the depressions usually bring the patient in. **Cyclothymic disorder**: at least 2 years (1 year in the young) of hypomanic and depressive symptoms that never meet full criteria. Mania that starts on an antidepressant and outlasts the drug\'s effect counts as bipolar disorder.',
'Treatment. **Acute mania**: an antipsychotic (haloperidol, olanzapine, quetiapine, risperidone, aripiprazole) with or without lithium or valproate; short-term benzodiazepine for agitation; stop antidepressants. **Maintenance**: lithium has the strongest evidence and lowers suicide; valproate, quetiapine, and lamotrigine (which mostly prevents the depressive pole). **Bipolar depression**: quetiapine, lurasidone, cariprazine, lumateperone or lamotrigine; never an antidepressant alone.'],
table:{cols:['Mood stabiliser','Mechanism and handling','Adverse effects and monitoring'],rows:[
['Lithium','inhibits inositol monophosphatase (lowers inositol recycling) and GSK-3; not metabolised, cleared by the kidney and reabsorbed with sodium in the proximal tubule; narrow window (maintenance about 0.6–1.0 mmol/L)','fine tremor, hypothyroidism and goitre, nephrogenic DI, hyperparathyroidism (raised Ca²⁺), weight gain, acne; Ebstein anomaly (small absolute risk); levels rise with thiazides, ACE inhibitors, ARBs, NSAIDs and dehydration; toxicity: coarse tremor, ataxia, confusion, seizures (haemodialysis if severe); check level, TSH, creatinine and calcium'],
['Valproate','blocks Na⁺ channels, raises GABA','neural tube defects and lower child IQ (avoid in anyone who could become pregnant), hepatotoxicity, pancreatitis, weight gain, tremor, thrombocytopenia, hyperammonaemia'],
['Lamotrigine','blocks Na⁺ channels, reduces glutamate release','Stevens-Johnson syndrome: titrate slowly, restart slowly after a gap; valproate doubles its level'],
['Carbamazepine','blocks Na⁺ channels','agranulocytosis and aplastic anaemia, SIADH, Stevens-Johnson (screen for HLA-B*15:02 in people of Asian ancestry), strong P450 inducer (lowers contraceptive levels)'],
['Atypical antipsychotics','D₂ and 5-HT₂A antagonists or partial agonists','quetiapine, olanzapine, risperidone, aripiprazole for mania; metabolic monitoring']]},
s:[['path','An antidepressant without a mood stabiliser can tip bipolar depression into mania or rapid cycling (four or more episodes a year).'],['bio','Why thiazides raise lithium: they cause mild volume loss, so the proximal tubule reabsorbs more sodium, and lithium rides along with it. Any state of sodium and water loss (vomiting, diarrhoea, heat, low-salt diets) does the same.']]},
{h:'Anxiety, trauma and obsession',p:[
'**Generalised anxiety disorder**: excessive, hard-to-control worry about many things on most days for at least **6 months**, with at least 3 of restlessness, fatigue, poor concentration, irritability, muscle tension and poor sleep. Treat with CBT, SSRIs or SNRIs, and **buspirone** (5-HT₁A partial agonist: slow onset, no sedation, no dependence, no use in panic). **Panic disorder**: recurrent unexpected attacks peaking within minutes (palpitations, chest pain, breathlessness, sweating, fear of dying), followed by at least **1 month** of worry about more attacks or avoidance; CBT and SSRIs, benzodiazepines only briefly. Agoraphobia is a separate diagnosis. **Specific phobia** and **social anxiety disorder** last at least 6 months; exposure-based CBT, SSRIs for social anxiety, and a β-blocker taken before a performance when the fear is only of performing.',
'**Obsessive-compulsive disorder**: intrusive, unwanted thoughts (obsessions) and repetitive acts done to relieve them (compulsions), time-consuming (often more than an hour a day) or distressing; the patient usually sees them as senseless (ego-dystonic). Treat with exposure and response prevention, high-dose SSRIs or clomipramine. Related disorders: body dysmorphic disorder, hoarding, trichotillomania, excoriation; tics and Tourette syndrome often coexist.',
'**Trauma and stress**. Time sorts the diagnosis. **Acute stress disorder**: intrusion, avoidance, negative mood, dissociation and arousal from 3 days to 1 month after a trauma. **PTSD**: the same picture lasting **more than 1 month** (intrusions and nightmares, avoidance, negative thoughts and mood, hyperarousal and startle); trauma-focused CBT or EMDR first, SSRIs or venlafaxine, prazosin sometimes for nightmares; benzodiazepines worsen outcomes. **Adjustment disorder**: emotional or behavioural symptoms within 3 months of a non-traumatic stressor (divorce, illness, job loss), out of proportion to it, and gone within 6 months of the stressor ending. Separation anxiety and the eating disorders are in the Small Rooms.'],
table:{cols:['Diagnosis','Time rule','Clue'],rows:[
['Acute stress disorder','3 days to 1 month after trauma','dissociation prominent'],
['PTSD','more than 1 month after trauma','intrusions, avoidance, hyperarousal'],
['Adjustment disorder','starts within 3 months of a stressor; ends within 6 months of its end','stressor not life-threatening'],
['Panic disorder','1 month of worry after attacks','attacks unexpected'],
['GAD, phobias, social anxiety','6 months','worry about many things / one feared thing'],
['Major depressive episode','2 weeks','5 of 9'],
['Hypomania / mania','4 days / 1 week','no impairment / marked impairment'],
['Persistent depressive, cyclothymic','2 years (1 in children)','never well for more than 2 months']]},
s:[['pharm','Benzodiazepines bind between the α and γ subunits of GABA-A and increase the **frequency** of chloride channel opening; barbiturates increase the **duration** and, at high doses, open the channel themselves, which is why they kill in overdose and benzodiazepines alone rarely do. Short-acting agents (alprazolam, triazolam) cause more rebound and dependence. In older adults they cause falls and delirium. Overdose: support breathing; flumazenil only in benzodiazepine-naive patients, because it can trigger seizures in dependence. Abrupt withdrawal can cause seizures.']]}
],
Q:[
{q:'A man on phenelzine eats aged cheese and develops a severe occipital headache with BP 220/120. Mechanism?',o:['Serotonin syndrome with clonus','Anticholinergic toxicity with dry, hot skin','Lithium toxicity from dehydration','Tyramine-driven noradrenaline release'],a:3,d:'pharm',c:'catecholamines',x:'MAO inhibition lets dietary tyramine reach sympathetic nerve endings, where it displaces stored noradrenaline and causes a hypertensive crisis. Serotonin syndrome is the tempting choice, but it needs a second serotonergic drug and shows clonus and hyperthermia rather than a pure pressor surge.'},
{q:'A patient stable on lithium starts hydrochlorothiazide and develops coarse tremor and confusion. Why?',o:['Thiazides raise the serum lithium level','Thiazides cause hyponatraemia only','Thiazides block lithium uptake','Thiazides cause hypercalcaemia'],a:0,d:'pharm',c:'mood-disorders',x:'Thiazide-induced volume loss makes the proximal tubule reabsorb more sodium, and lithium with it, so the level climbs into the toxic range. Hyponatraemia can occur on thiazides, but it is the raised lithium level that explains coarse tremor and confusion.'},
{q:'Which antidepressant is best avoided in bulimia nervosa because it lowers the seizure threshold?',o:['Sertraline','Mirtazapine','Bupropion','Fluoxetine'],a:2,d:'pharm',c:'mood-disorders',x:'Bupropion lowers the seizure threshold, and purging adds electrolyte disturbance that lowers it further. Fluoxetine is not a trap here: it is the SSRI best studied in bulimia.'},
{q:'Hyperthermia, ankle clonus and agitation a few hours after tramadol is added to sertraline. Specific antidote?',o:['Dantrolene','Bromocriptine','Methylene blue','Cyproheptadine'],a:3,d:'pharm',c:'toxicology',x:'This is serotonin syndrome, and cyproheptadine blocks 5-HT₂A receptors after stopping the drugs, cooling and giving benzodiazepines. Dantrolene and bromocriptine belong to malignant hyperthermia and neuroleptic malignant syndrome, which show rigidity rather than clonus.'},
{q:'Minimum duration of symptoms for a manic episode when no admission is needed?',o:['4 days','2 weeks','1 week','1 month'],a:2,d:'path',c:'mood-disorders',x:'Mania needs at least 1 week of symptoms, or any duration if the patient has to be admitted. Four days is the threshold for hypomania, which by definition causes no marked impairment.'},
{q:'For 3 weeks a man has had insomnia, poor appetite, fatigue and poor concentration, but his mood is normal and he still enjoys his hobbies. Does he meet criteria for major depression?',o:['Yes: four symptoms for over 2 weeks','No: low mood or anhedonia is required','Yes: duration alone is enough','No: symptoms must last 6 months'],a:1,d:'path',c:'mood-disorders',x:'Major depression needs at least five symptoms, and one must be depressed mood or anhedonia; he has four and neither gate symptom. The duration of over 2 weeks is met, which is why the first option tempts, but the count and the core symptom are both missing.'},
{q:'Ten days after delivery a mother is tearful for a few hours each day but sleeps when the baby sleeps, bonds well and has no thoughts of harm. Best step?',o:['Reassurance and review within 2 weeks','Start sertraline','Start zuranolone','Urgent psychiatric admission'],a:0,d:'path',c:'mood-disorders',x:'Mild, brief tearfulness in the first two weeks with intact function is postpartum blues, which resolves on its own. Drugs such as sertraline or zuranolone are for a full depressive episode, and admission is for postpartum psychosis.'}
]};

BOOK['c15-psychosis']={depth:15,title:'The False Voices',
epi:'Some voices here speak in the first person. Count them. There should be only one.',
c:['psychosis','catecholamines','parkinson'],
S:[
{h:'Psychosis',p:[
'Psychosis is loss of contact with reality: **hallucinations** (auditory are commonest in schizophrenia; visual, olfactory or tactile ones point to delirium, drugs, seizures or other organic disease), **delusions** (fixed false beliefs held despite clear evidence, not explained by culture), and **disorganised** speech and behaviour. **Schizophrenia** (DSM-5-TR): at least **2 of 5** core symptoms (delusions, hallucinations, disorganised speech, grossly disorganised or catatonic behaviour, negative symptoms), at least one of them from the first three, present for a significant part of **1 month**, within **6 months** of continuous signs of illness (including prodromal or residual phases), with a fall in work, social or self-care function. **Positive** symptoms: hallucinations, delusions, disorganisation. **Negative** symptoms: flat affect, alogia, avolition, anhedonia, asociality; they respond poorly to drugs and drive long-term disability. Cognitive impairment (working memory, attention) is common.',
'Duration and mood sort the diagnosis. **Brief psychotic disorder**: from 1 day to under 1 month, then full return to baseline, often after a stressor. **Schizophreniform disorder**: 1 to 6 months. **Schizophrenia**: 6 months or more. **Schizoaffective disorder**: mood episodes present for most of the illness, plus at least 2 weeks of delusions or hallucinations **without** a mood episode; in a **mood disorder with psychotic features**, by contrast, the psychosis appears only inside mood episodes. **Delusional disorder**: one or more delusions for at least 1 month, criteria for schizophrenia never met, and function not markedly impaired apart from the delusion; DSM-5-TR no longer requires the delusion to be non-bizarre. Catatonia (stupor, posturing, mutism, waxy flexibility) can complicate psychosis, mood disorders or medical illness; lorazepam first, then ECT.',
'Biology. Dopamine excess in the mesolimbic pathway drives positive symptoms; a deficit in the mesocortical pathway contributes to negative and cognitive ones. NMDA receptor hypofunction also matters: PCP and ketamine, which block NMDA receptors, reproduce both positive and negative symptoms. Imaging shows enlarged lateral ventricles and reduced cortical and hippocampal volume. Risk: family history (about 10% with one affected parent, about 50% concordance in identical twins), heavy cannabis use in adolescence, obstetric complications, late-winter birth, urban upbringing, migration. Onset is earlier in men (late teens to mid 20s) than women (late 20s). Better prognosis: late or sudden onset, a clear trigger, mood symptoms, mainly positive symptoms, good premorbid function.'],
table:{cols:['Dopamine pathway','Route','When blocked or out of balance'],rows:[
['Mesolimbic','ventral tegmental area → nucleus accumbens, limbic system','excess → positive symptoms; blocking it is the antipsychotic effect; also the reward pathway'],
['Mesocortical','ventral tegmental area → prefrontal cortex','too little → negative and cognitive symptoms; D₂ block can worsen them'],
['Nigrostriatal','substantia nigra pars compacta → striatum','D₂ block → extrapyramidal effects (dystonia, akathisia, parkinsonism, tardive dyskinesia)'],
['Tuberoinfundibular','arcuate nucleus → pituitary portal vessels','dopamine normally inhibits prolactin; D₂ block → hyperprolactinaemia']]},
s:[['path','Always exclude organic causes first: **delirium** (acute, fluctuating attention; common in hospital), drugs (amphetamines, cocaine, cannabis, PCP, ketamine, steroids, levodopa, anticholinergics) and withdrawal, temporal lobe epilepsy, tumours, encephalitis (**anti-NMDA receptor encephalitis** in young women, often with an ovarian teratoma: psychosis, seizures, orofacial dyskinesia, autonomic instability), thyroid disease, Cushing, B12 deficiency, HIV, neurosyphilis, Wilson disease, lupus.']]},
{h:'Antipsychotics',p:[
'**Typical** (first-generation) antipsychotics block **D₂**. High potency (haloperidol, fluphenazine) cause more extrapyramidal effects; low potency (chlorpromazine, thioridazine) cause more anticholinergic, antihistamine and α₁-blocking effects (dry mouth, sedation, orthostasis). Chlorpromazine leaves corneal deposits, thioridazine retinal pigmentation. **Atypical** (second-generation) agents block D₂ and 5-HT₂A, with fewer extrapyramidal effects but more metabolic harm: **risperidone** and paliperidone (most prolactin rise of the group), **olanzapine** and clozapine (weight gain, diabetes, dyslipidaemia), **quetiapine** (sedation, orthostasis), **ziprasidone** (QT prolongation), **aripiprazole**, brexpiprazole and cariprazine (**partial D₂ agonists**: little prolactin rise, akathisia), lurasidone (take with food). Monitor weight, glucose and lipids on every atypical, and the ECG when QT risk is high.',
'**Clozapine** is the drug for treatment-resistant schizophrenia (two failed adequate trials) and for persistent suicidality, and it rarely causes tardive dyskinesia. Its price: **agranulocytosis** (regular neutrophil counts), seizures, myocarditis and cardiomyopathy, severe constipation and ileus, hypersalivation, weight gain. **Xanomeline-trospium** (approved 2024) is the first antipsychotic that does not block D₂: xanomeline stimulates central M₁ and M₄ muscarinic receptors, and trospium blocks muscarinic receptors outside the brain to limit nausea and other cholinergic effects; expect anticholinergic side effects (dry mouth, constipation, urinary retention). **Long-acting injections** (paliperidone palmitate, aripiprazole, haloperidol decanoate, fluphenazine decanoate) help when adherence fails.'],
table:{cols:['Effect','Timing','Treatment'],rows:[
['Acute dystonia (torticollis, oculogyric crisis, laryngospasm)','hours to days; young men','benztropine or diphenhydramine'],
['Akathisia (inner restlessness, cannot sit still)','days to weeks','lower the dose, propranolol, benzodiazepine'],
['Parkinsonism (bradykinesia, rigidity, tremor)','weeks','lower the dose, benztropine, amantadine'],
['Tardive dyskinesia (orofacial and limb movements)','months to years; may be permanent','stop or switch (clozapine); VMAT2 inhibitors valbenazine, deutetrabenazine'],
['Neuroleptic malignant syndrome','usually days after starting or raising a dose','stop the drug, cool, fluids, dantrolene, bromocriptine; fever, lead-pipe rigidity, autonomic instability, confusion, very high CK, myoglobinuria'],
['Hyperprolactinaemia','weeks to months','galactorrhoea, amenorrhoea, gynaecomastia, low libido, bone loss; switch to aripiprazole']]},
key:'Hours: dystonia. Days: akathisia. Weeks: parkinsonism. Months to years: tardive dyskinesia. Any time, with fever and rigidity: neuroleptic malignant syndrome.',
s:[['pharm','Three hot, confused patients. **Neuroleptic malignant syndrome**: dopamine blockade (or abrupt levodopa withdrawal), onset over days, lead-pipe rigidity, slow reflexes, CK in the thousands. **Serotonin syndrome**: serotonergic drugs, hours, clonus and hyperreflexia, diarrhoea. **Malignant hyperthermia**: volatile anaesthetic or suxamethonium in a susceptible patient (RYR1), minutes, masseter rigidity and rising end-tidal CO₂; dantrolene.']]},
{h:'Personality disorders',p:[
'A **personality disorder** is an enduring, inflexible pattern of inner experience and behaviour that deviates from the person\'s culture, shows across many situations, starts by adolescence or early adulthood and causes distress or impairment. The traits usually feel natural to the person (ego-syntonic), which is why they rarely seek help for the traits themselves. Antisocial personality disorder needs age 18 or older plus evidence of conduct disorder before 15. Psychotherapy is the main treatment: **dialectical behaviour therapy** for borderline personality disorder, with drugs only for target symptoms.'],
table:{cols:['Cluster','Disorder','Core pattern'],rows:[
['A: odd','Paranoid','pervasive distrust; reads hidden threats into remarks; bears grudges'],
['A: odd','Schizoid','detached, prefers solitude, flat emotion, no wish for close relationships'],
['A: odd','Schizotypal','odd beliefs and magical thinking, eccentric speech and dress, social anxiety; on the schizophrenia spectrum'],
['B: dramatic','Antisocial','disregard for others\' rights, deceit, no remorse; conduct disorder before 15; more in men'],
['B: dramatic','Borderline','unstable relationships, self-image and mood; fear of abandonment, impulsivity, self-harm, chronic emptiness, splitting'],
['B: dramatic','Histrionic','attention-seeking, seductive, shallow and shifting emotion'],
['B: dramatic','Narcissistic','grandiose, needs admiration, lacks empathy; reacts to criticism with rage'],
['C: anxious','Avoidant','wants relationships but avoids them for fear of rejection; feels inadequate'],
['C: anxious','Dependent','needs to be cared for; submissive, cannot decide alone; may stay in abusive relationships'],
['C: anxious','Obsessive-compulsive','perfectionism, order and control at the cost of flexibility; ego-syntonic, unlike OCD']]},
s:[['phys','Ego defences are tested by definition. Immature: **denial** (refusing to accept a painful fact), **projection** (attributing one\'s own unacceptable impulse to someone else), **displacement** (redirecting feeling onto a safer target), **splitting** (people are all good or all bad; borderline PD), **reaction formation** (acting the opposite of an unacceptable feeling), **regression** (returning to childish behaviour under stress), **rationalisation** (logical-sounding excuses for a failure), **intellectualisation** (abstract talk to avoid feeling), **acting out**, **dissociation**. Mature: **sublimation** (channelling an impulse into a valued activity), **altruism**, **humour**, **suppression** (consciously postponing a thought).']]},
{h:'When the body speaks',p:[
'Two questions sort the disorders where the body carries the complaint: are the symptoms produced on purpose, and if so, why? **Somatic symptom disorder**: one or more real or unexplained symptoms with excessive thoughts, worry and time devoted to them for at least 6 months. **Illness anxiety disorder**: fear of having a serious illness with few or no symptoms. **Functional neurological symptom disorder** (conversion disorder): weakness, non-epileptic attacks, blindness or numbness inconsistent with neurological disease (Hoover sign, give-way weakness), often after stress; the patient is not faking. In none of these are symptoms made on purpose. **Factitious disorder imposed on self** (formerly Munchausen syndrome): the person deliberately fakes or induces illness to occupy the sick role, with no external reward. **Factitious disorder imposed on another** (formerly by proxy): a caregiver induces illness in a dependant, usually a child; this is abuse and must be reported. **Malingering** is not a mental disorder: symptoms are faked for an external gain (money, drugs, time off, avoiding prison) and stop when the gain is reached.'],
table:{cols:['Disorder','Made on purpose?','Motive'],rows:[
['Somatic symptom, illness anxiety, functional neurological','no','none conscious'],
['Factitious disorder (on self or another)','yes','the sick role itself'],
['Malingering','yes','external reward']]},
s:[['path','Factitious clues: a healthcare worker or carer with dramatic, unexplained episodes and many admissions. Self-injected insulin gives hypoglycaemia with **high insulin and low C-peptide**; a sulfonylurea gives high insulin and high C-peptide with a positive drug screen, mimicking insulinoma.']]}
],
Q:[
{q:'Schizophrenia has not responded to two antipsychotics, and the patient has made two suicide attempts. Drug and essential monitoring?',o:['Haloperidol; ECG for the QT','Aripiprazole; prolactin level','Risperidone; weight and glucose','Clozapine; absolute neutrophil count'],a:3,d:'pharm',c:'psychosis',x:'Clozapine is the drug for treatment-resistant schizophrenia and the one shown to reduce suicide. It can cause agranulocytosis, so neutrophil counts are checked regularly; the other pairs are real monitoring links but not for resistant disease.'},
{q:'Hours after IM haloperidol a young man\'s neck twists to one side and his eyes roll upward. Treatment?',o:['Benztropine','Dantrolene','Propranolol','Valbenazine'],a:0,d:'pharm',c:'parkinson',x:'Acute dystonia appears within hours to days of a high-potency D₂ blocker and responds to an anticholinergic such as benztropine or diphenhydramine. Valbenazine is for tardive dyskinesia, which takes months to years to develop.'},
{q:'Fever, lead-pipe rigidity, CK 18 000 U/L and swinging blood pressure four days after starting an antipsychotic. Diagnosis?',o:['Serotonin syndrome after a drug interaction','Malignant hyperthermia','Neuroleptic malignant syndrome','Catatonia'],a:2,d:'pharm',c:'psychosis',x:'Neuroleptic malignant syndrome develops over days after a dopamine blocker and combines rigidity, fever, autonomic instability and a very high CK. Serotonin syndrome is faster and shows clonus and hyperreflexia instead of lead-pipe rigidity.'},
{q:'Delusions and hallucinations for 3 months, with no mood episodes and no drug use. Diagnosis?',o:['Brief psychotic disorder','Schizophreniform disorder','Schizophrenia','Schizoaffective disorder, depressive type'],a:1,d:'path',c:'psychosis',x:'Psychosis lasting 1 to 6 months is schizophreniform disorder. Schizophrenia needs 6 months of signs, and schizoaffective disorder needs mood episodes for most of the illness.'},
{q:'A 24-year-old woman develops psychosis, seizures and orofacial dyskinesia; pelvic imaging shows an ovarian teratoma. Antibody?',o:['Anti-Hu','Anti-Yo','Anti-voltage-gated calcium channel','Anti-NMDA receptor'],a:3,d:'path',c:'psychosis',x:'Anti-NMDA receptor encephalitis is a paraneoplastic or autoimmune encephalitis, classically with an ovarian teratoma, and improves with tumour removal and immunotherapy. Anti-Hu and anti-Yo go with small cell lung cancer and gynaecological or breast cancers, causing encephalomyelitis and cerebellar degeneration.'},
{q:'A 50-year-old accountant has believed for 5 months that her neighbour poisons her water supply. She works normally and has no hallucinations or mood symptoms. Diagnosis?',o:['Delusional disorder','Schizophrenia','Paranoid personality disorder','Schizophreniform disorder'],a:0,d:'path',c:'psychosis',x:'A delusion lasting at least a month, with schizophrenia criteria never met and function otherwise preserved, is delusional disorder. Paranoid personality disorder is a lifelong pattern of distrust, not a fixed false belief.'},
{q:'A hospital worker has repeated hypoglycaemia; insulin is high and C-peptide is low. She seeks no money or time off. Diagnosis?',o:['Malingering','Factitious disorder imposed on self','Illness anxiety disorder','Insulin-secreting tumour (insulinoma)'],a:1,d:'path',c:'diabetes',x:'High insulin with low C-peptide means insulin from outside the body, and inducing illness with no external reward is factitious disorder. Malingering needs an external gain, and an insulinoma would raise C-peptide along with insulin.'}
]};

BOOK['c15-sleep']={depth:15,title:'The Night Ward',
epi:'The night ward. 03:17 on every clock. Something happened here that you have not let yourself remember.',
c:['sleep','parkinson','catecholamines'],
S:[
{h:'Sleep architecture',p:[
'Sleep runs in cycles of about **90 minutes**, four to six a night, each passing through the NREM stages into **REM**. Slow-wave sleep (N3) dominates the first cycles, and REM periods grow longer toward morning. With age, N3 shrinks, sleep fragments and the clock advances. Growth hormone is released in N3, and many patients with depression enter REM unusually early.',
'Arousal is a chorus: noradrenaline (locus coeruleus), serotonin (raphe), histamine (tuberomammillary nucleus), acetylcholine (pons and basal forebrain) and **orexin** (lateral hypothalamus), which holds the system steadily awake. Sleep comes when GABA neurons of the **ventrolateral preoptic nucleus** switch these off, helped by **adenosine** that accumulates with every waking hour (caffeine blocks adenosine receptors). REM is a cholinergic state: pontine acetylcholine is high while noradrenaline and serotonin fall almost silent.',
'The **suprachiasmatic nucleus** of the hypothalamus is the master clock. Light reaching the retina travels by the retinohypothalamic tract and resets it each morning; in darkness the SCN signals, through a long sympathetic loop (spinal cord, superior cervical ganglion), for the pineal gland to make **melatonin**, and light shuts that signal off. Night-shift work drags waking against this clock: poor sleep, raised metabolic and cardiovascular risk, mood problems, and the most errors at the bottom of the circadian trough, about 03:00 to 05:00.'],
table:{cols:['Stage','EEG','What happens'],rows:[
['Awake','beta (eyes open, alert); alpha (eyes closed, relaxed)','—'],
['N1 (about 5%)','theta','lightest sleep; hypnic jerks'],
['N2 (about 45–55%)','sleep spindles and K-complexes','most of the night; bruxism'],
['N3 (about 15–20%)','delta, slow and high','deepest; sleepwalking, night terrors, bedwetting; growth hormone pulse; mostly early in the night'],
['REM (about 20–25%)','fast, low-voltage, sawtooth waves','dreams and nightmares, atonia of skeletal muscle, rapid eye movements (PPRF), penile and clitoral tumescence, variable pulse and blood pressure; longer toward morning']]},
key:'Spindles and K-complexes: N2. Delta: N3, home of sleepwalking and night terrors. Sawtooth waves and a paralysed body: REM.',
s:[['pharm','Benzodiazepines and alcohol reduce N3 and REM; many antidepressants suppress REM. **Z-drugs** (zolpidem, zaleplon, eszopiclone) act at the benzodiazepine site of GABA-A (complex sleep behaviours such as sleep-driving). **Melatonin agonists** (ramelteon) help sleep onset without dependence. **Dual orexin receptor antagonists** (suvorexant, lemborexant, daridorexant) turn the wake signal down; avoid in narcolepsy. Sedating antihistamines and trazodone are common but weak choices, and anticholinergic hypnotics cause delirium in older adults.']]},
{h:'Sleep disorders',p:[
'**Narcolepsy**: excessive daytime sleepiness with sudden sleep attacks, **cataplexy** (brief loss of muscle tone triggered by laughter or surprise, consciousness kept), hypnagogic and hypnopompic hallucinations, sleep paralysis, and fragmented night sleep. Type 1 is loss of **orexin (hypocretin)** neurons in the lateral hypothalamus (low CSF orexin, strong HLA-DQB1*06:02 link). Testing: multiple sleep latency test with short mean sleep latency and **sleep-onset REM periods**. Treat with scheduled naps, modafinil or armodafinil, solriamfetol, pitolisant (histamine H₃ inverse agonist) or stimulants; **sodium oxybate** (GHB) for cataplexy and sleep.',
'**Insomnia disorder**: trouble starting or keeping sleep at least 3 nights a week for 3 months despite the chance to sleep. **CBT for insomnia** (sleep restriction, stimulus control, sleep hygiene) is first line; hypnotics only short term. **Circadian rhythm disorders**: delayed sleep phase (adolescents: cannot fall asleep before 02:00, then cannot wake), advanced sleep phase (older adults), shift-work disorder; timed light and melatonin reset the clock. **Obstructive sleep apnoea** is in the Alveolar Caves; **central sleep apnoea** (no breathing effort) comes with heart failure (Cheyne-Stokes breathing), opioids and brainstem lesions.',
'**Restless legs syndrome**: an urge to move the legs, worse at rest and in the evening, relieved by moving; check ferritin (iron deficiency), and think of CKD and pregnancy. Treat with iron when ferritin is low, then gabapentinoids; dopamine agonists work but cause augmentation over time. **Parasomnias**: NREM ones (sleepwalking, night terrors) come in the first third of the night with no memory of them; REM ones (nightmares) come later with vivid recall. **REM sleep behaviour disorder**: loss of REM atonia, so the sleeper acts out violent dreams; it can precede Parkinson disease, Lewy body dementia or multiple system atrophy by years (clonazepam, melatonin).'],
s:[['path','**Delirium** is an acute, fluctuating disturbance of attention and awareness, often worse at night: infections, drugs (anticholinergics, benzodiazepines, opioids), alcohol or sedative withdrawal, hypoxia, metabolic disturbance, urinary retention, constipation and sleep loss in hospital. EEG shows diffuse slowing. Find and treat the cause, reorient, keep a day-night routine, avoid sedatives; low-dose haloperidol only if the patient endangers themselves or others. It is usually reversible, unlike dementia, but both often coexist.']]},
{h:'Grief and memory',p:[
'**Normal grief**: shock, longing, sadness, guilt, sleep loss, even brief hallucinations of the dead person\'s voice or presence; it comes in waves and eases over months, while the person can still accept comfort. **Prolonged grief disorder** (DSM-5-TR): at least **12 months** after a death in adults (6 months in children), intense yearning for or preoccupation with the dead person nearly every day, plus at least 3 of identity disruption, marked disbelief, avoidance of reminders, intense emotional pain, trouble re-engaging with life, numbness, a sense that life is meaningless, and intense loneliness, with real impairment; grief-focused psychotherapy helps. Depression after a loss is diagnosed whenever the full criteria for a major depressive episode are met; there is no longer a bereavement exclusion.',
'Traumatic events can leave **dissociative amnesia** (inability to recall important personal information, usually about the trauma, sometimes with dissociative fugue), **depersonalisation or derealisation disorder** (feeling detached from oneself or that the world is unreal, with reality testing intact), or, after severe repeated childhood trauma, **dissociative identity disorder**. Memories are consolidated from the **hippocampus** into the cortex during sleep, and emotional memories are strengthened by the **amygdala** through noradrenaline: what was frightening is remembered vividly, or walled off altogether.',
'**Anterograde amnesia** is the loss of new learning after an injury; **retrograde amnesia** is the loss of memories formed before it, usually the most recent ones first. Bilateral hippocampal damage (HSV encephalitis, hypoxia, temporal lobe surgery) abolishes new long-term memories while skills and old memories remain. **Transient global amnesia** is a sudden attack of anterograde amnesia lasting hours, with repeated questions and full recovery.'],
s:[['phys','**Korsakoff syndrome** (chronic thiamine deficiency, damage to the mammillary bodies and medial thalamus): severe anterograde and some retrograde amnesia with confabulation; usually permanent. It often follows untreated **Wernicke encephalopathy** (confusion, ophthalmoplegia with nystagmus, ataxia), which is why thiamine is given before glucose.']]}
],
Q:[
{q:'Sleep spindles and K-complexes mark which stage?',o:['N1','N2','N3','REM'],a:1,d:'phys',c:'sleep',x:'Spindles and K-complexes define N2, the stage that fills about half the night. N3 is the tempting choice because it is also NREM, but it is defined by slow, high delta waves.'},
{q:'A 7-year-old screams and thrashes an hour after falling asleep, cannot be comforted and remembers nothing in the morning. Sleep stage?',o:['N1','REM','N3','N2'],a:2,d:'phys',c:'sleep',x:'Night terrors arise from slow-wave sleep (N3), which dominates the early night in children, and leave no memory. Nightmares come from REM, later in the night, and are vividly recalled.'},
{q:'Daytime sleep attacks and sudden knee buckling when laughing. Which neuropeptide is deficient?',o:['Melatonin','Serotonin','Histamine','Orexin'],a:3,d:'path',c:'sleep',x:'Narcolepsy with cataplexy is caused by loss of orexin neurons in the lateral hypothalamus. Histamine neurons are part of the wake system too, and pitolisant works through them, but they are not the ones lost.'},
{q:'A 68-year-old man punches and kicks during vivid dreams; his wife now sleeps in another room. Which disease may follow?',o:['Parkinson disease','Alzheimer disease','Huntington disease','Multiple sclerosis'],a:0,d:'path',c:'parkinson',x:'REM sleep behaviour disorder is often the first sign of a synucleinopathy and can precede Parkinson disease, Lewy body dementia or multiple system atrophy by years. Alzheimer disease, a tauopathy and amyloid disease, has no such link.'},
{q:'Which structure is the master circadian clock?',o:['Pineal gland','Ventromedial hypothalamic nucleus','Suprachiasmatic nucleus','Mammillary body'],a:2,d:'phys',c:'sleep',x:'The suprachiasmatic nucleus receives light through the retinohypothalamic tract and times the pineal gland\'s melatonin release. The pineal gland is the tempting answer, but it only follows the clock\'s orders.'},
{q:'Fourteen months after his wife died, a man still yearns for her daily, cannot accept the death and has stopped working. He does not meet criteria for depression. Diagnosis?',o:['Normal grief','Prolonged grief disorder','Adjustment disorder','Persistent depressive disorder'],a:1,d:'path',c:'mood-disorders',x:'Intense yearning with impairment more than 12 months after a death is prolonged grief disorder (DSM-5-TR). Adjustment disorder applies to non-death stressors and must end within 6 months of the stressor ending.'},
{q:'Which hypnotic works by blocking the receptors for the neuropeptide lost in narcolepsy?',o:['Suvorexant','Eszopiclone','Ramelteon','Trazodone'],a:0,d:'pharm',c:'sleep',x:'Suvorexant and the other dual orexin receptor antagonists turn the orexin wake signal down. Zolpidem acts at the benzodiazepine site of GABA-A, and ramelteon at melatonin receptors.'}
]};

BOOK['c15-subst']={depth:15,title:'The Craving Pools',
epi:'Dark pools that pull. Down at the bottom of the last one, a memory is waiting to surface.',
c:['substance-use','ethanol','toxicology','catecholamines'],
S:[
{h:'Reward and dependence',p:[
'Every addictive drug, whatever its receptor, ends by raising **dopamine** in the **mesolimbic** pathway, from the ventral tegmental area to the nucleus accumbens. Repeated surges teach the brain to value the drug cue above natural rewards, while the prefrontal brakes weaken. **Substance use disorder** (DSM-5-TR): at least **2 of 11** criteria within 12 months, across four groups: impaired control (more than intended, failed cutting down, much time spent, craving), social impairment (failed duties, conflict, giving up activities), risky use (dangerous situations, use despite known harm), and pharmacological signs (tolerance, withdrawal). Severity: mild 2–3, moderate 4–5, severe 6 or more. Tolerance and withdrawal from a drug taken as prescribed do not count.',
'**Stages of change**: precontemplation (no intention), contemplation (ambivalent), preparation, action, maintenance, and relapse, which is common and not a failure. **Motivational interviewing** meets patients where they are: open questions, reflective listening, and drawing out their own reasons to change. Screening tools ask about cutting down, criticism by others, guilt and morning use (alcohol), or score quantity and harm (AUDIT).'],
table:{cols:['Substance','Intoxication','Withdrawal'],rows:[
['Alcohol','disinhibition, slurred speech, ataxia, nystagmus, coma, respiratory depression','tremor, sweating, tachycardia, anxiety at 6–24 h; hallucinosis (clear consciousness) 12–48 h; seizures 12–48 h; **delirium tremens** 48–96 h (confusion, hallucinations, fever, autonomic storm; can kill); benzodiazepines'],
['Opioids','euphoria, pinpoint pupils, respiratory depression, constipation; naloxone (repeat doses for fentanyl and long-acting opioids)','dilated pupils, yawning, lacrimation, rhinorrhoea, piloerection, diarrhoea, cramps; miserable but rarely dangerous; methadone, buprenorphine, lofexidine or clonidine (α₂ agonists)'],
['Benzodiazepines','sedation, ataxia; respiratory depression with alcohol or opioids; flumazenil (caution)','anxiety, tremor, insomnia, **seizures**, delirium; taper slowly'],
['Barbiturates','sedation, respiratory depression, coma; no antidote','like alcohol and as dangerous: seizures, delirium, cardiovascular collapse'],
['GHB','euphoria, sudden deep coma with abrupt waking, bradycardia','severe, alcohol-like withdrawal with delirium'],
['Cocaine','euphoria, dilated pupils, hypertension, tachycardia, MI (vasospasm), stroke, seizures, hyperthermia, psychosis, tactile hallucinations ("bugs"); benzodiazepines first, avoid a β-blocker alone','crash: depression, hypersomnia, hunger, craving'],
['Amphetamines, MDMA','like cocaine but longer; methamphetamine: dental decay; MDMA: hyperthermia, bruxism, hyponatraemia (ADH release, heavy water intake), serotonin syndrome','depression, fatigue, hypersomnia'],
['Cannabis','euphoria, red eyes, dry mouth, hunger, slowed reaction, anxiety and paranoia; heavy use: hyperemesis syndrome relieved by hot showers','irritability, poor sleep, low appetite'],
['PCP, ketamine','dissociation, violence (PCP), vertical and horizontal nystagmus, analgesia, psychosis; chronic ketamine: ulcerative cystitis','mild'],
['LSD, psilocybin','perceptual distortion, synaesthesia, dilated pupils; flashbacks (hallucinogen persisting perception disorder)','none significant'],
['Inhalants (solvents, glues)','euphoria, slurred speech, ataxia; sudden death from arrhythmia; chronic: neuropathy, white-matter damage','mild irritability'],
['Nicotine, caffeine','alertness','nicotine: irritability, hunger, craving (varenicline, bupropion, replacement); caffeine: headache, fatigue']]},
key:'Withdrawal that can kill: alcohol, benzodiazepines, barbiturates and GHB (seizures, delirium). Opioid withdrawal is miserable but rarely fatal; with opioids it is the overdose that kills.',
s:[['phys','Pupils in the dark pools: pinpoint with opioids (and pontine lesions); dilated with stimulants, hallucinogens, anticholinergics and opioid withdrawal. Vertical nystagmus in an agitated patient points to PCP.']]},
{h:'Alcohol in detail',p:[
'Alcohol is oxidised by **alcohol dehydrogenase** (saturated at low levels, so elimination is zero-order, about one drink an hour) and then **aldehyde dehydrogenase**; CYP2E1 is induced by chronic drinking. Both steps turn NAD⁺ into **NADH**, and the high NADH:NAD⁺ ratio pushes pyruvate to lactate and oxaloacetate to malate. This blocks gluconeogenesis (**fasting hypoglycaemia**), raises lactate (lactic acidosis), drives fatty acid synthesis and blocks β-oxidation (**fatty liver**), and favours ketone formation (alcoholic ketoacidosis). East Asian variants of aldehyde dehydrogenase cause flushing.',
'Chronic use damages almost every organ: alcoholic liver disease and cirrhosis, pancreatitis, dilated cardiomyopathy, atrial fibrillation ("holiday heart"), macrocytosis, gastritis, Mallory-Weiss tears and varices, cancers of the mouth, oesophagus, liver and breast, fetal alcohol syndrome. In the nervous system: **Wernicke encephalopathy** (confusion, ophthalmoplegia, ataxia; give IV thiamine before glucose), **Korsakoff syndrome**, cerebellar vermis degeneration (truncal ataxia), peripheral neuropathy, and osmotic demyelination if hyponatraemia is corrected too fast.',
'Withdrawal is scored by symptoms (CIWA) and treated with benzodiazepines (long-acting chlordiazepoxide or diazepam; lorazepam or oxazepam in liver failure or old age), thiamine, fluids and electrolytes (magnesium, phosphate). Relapse prevention: **naltrexone** (opioid antagonist, reduces craving and the pleasure of drinking; not with opioids or in acute hepatitis), **acamprosate** (glutamate/NMDA modulation; cleared by the kidney, so dose by renal function, fine in liver disease), **disulfiram** (aldehyde dehydrogenase inhibitor: acetaldehyde builds up after a drink, causing flushing, vomiting and hypotension; needs full commitment). Topiramate and gabapentin are used off-label. Metronidazole and some cephalosporins also cause a disulfiram-like reaction.'],
s:[['pharm','Opioid use disorder. **Methadone**: long-acting full μ agonist, once daily, QT prolongation. **Buprenorphine**: high-affinity **partial** agonist with a ceiling on respiratory depression; given too soon after heroin or fentanyl it displaces the full agonist and precipitates withdrawal, so start when withdrawal has begun (often combined with naloxone to deter injection). **Naltrexone** (oral or monthly injection) after full detoxification. Naloxone kits for patients and families save lives; long-acting opioids can outlast a single naloxone dose, so observe. **Lofexidine** (α₂ agonist) eases withdrawal symptoms.']]},
{h:'Exposure at work',p:[
'Healthcare workers carry particular risks: night shifts, fatigue, access to controlled drugs (opioids, propofol, fentanyl, benzodiazepines), burnout, and occupational exposure to blood. Burnout (emotional exhaustion, depersonalisation, a sense of reduced accomplishment) raises error rates and is linked to depression and substance use. Needlestick injuries go unreported in up to half of cases, often from fatigue, embarrassment or because the worker felt the risk was small; unreported injuries cannot receive post-exposure prophylaxis or follow-up testing.',
'A colleague who seems impaired at work (smelling of alcohol, missing drugs from a ward stock, unexplained absences) is a patient-safety problem and a person in need of help. The expected action is to report the concern through the proper channel (a supervisor, the department lead or a physician health programme), not to confront them alone, ignore it or cover for them. Physician health programmes offer confidential treatment and monitored return to work, with high recovery rates.'],
s:[['phys','Under sustained stress, cortisol rhythm flattens, sleep fragments, and attention narrows. The worst hour for errors is at the bottom of the circadian trough, in the small hours of a night shift.'],['micro','Every sharps injury follows the same order: wash with soap and water, report at once, test the source and the worker. HIV post-exposure prophylaxis works best within hours and should start no later than 72 hours after exposure; hepatitis B depends on the worker\'s vaccine response; for hepatitis C there is no prophylaxis, only early testing and treatment.']]}
],
Q:[
{q:'Two days after admission, a patient with heavy alcohol use becomes confused, febrile, tachycardic and sees insects on the walls. Diagnosis and treatment?',o:['Wernicke encephalopathy; thiamine','Opioid withdrawal; methadone','Hepatic encephalopathy; lactulose','Delirium tremens; high-dose benzodiazepines'],a:3,d:'pharm',c:'substance-use',x:'Delirium tremens appears 48–96 hours after the last drink with confusion, hallucinations and autonomic overactivity, and needs high-dose benzodiazepines. Wernicke encephalopathy tempts because thiamine is also given, but it causes ophthalmoplegia and ataxia without the autonomic storm.'},
{q:'Dilated pupils, yawning, runny nose, piloerection and diarrhoea. Withdrawal from which drug?',o:['Opioids','Alcohol','Cocaine','Benzodiazepines'],a:0,d:'pharm',c:'substance-use',x:'Opioid withdrawal is the mirror image of intoxication: wide pupils, secretions, gooseflesh and gut overactivity. Alcohol and benzodiazepine withdrawal cause tremor and can cause seizures, which opioid withdrawal does not.'},
{q:'Chest pain and BP 200/120 after cocaine. Which drug should not be given on its own?',o:['Intravenous benzodiazepine','Nitrate','Non-selective β-blocker','Aspirin'],a:2,d:'pharm',c:'catecholamines',x:'A non-selective β-blocker alone leaves α-receptors unopposed, which can worsen vasospasm and hypertension. Benzodiazepines are the first-line drug, and nitrates and aspirin are given for the chest pain.'},
{q:'Why does a binge without food cause fasting hypoglycaemia?',o:['It stimulates pancreatic insulin release','High NADH blocks gluconeogenesis','It breaks down liver glycogen','It blocks glucagon receptors'],a:1,d:'bio',c:'ethanol',x:'Alcohol oxidation generates NADH, which converts pyruvate to lactate and oxaloacetate to malate, starving gluconeogenesis of substrate. Glycogen loss matters only because fasting has already emptied the stores; it does not cause the low glucose by itself.'},
{q:'Which drug for alcohol use disorder is an opioid receptor antagonist?',o:['Acamprosate','Disulfiram','Varenicline','Naltrexone'],a:3,d:'pharm',c:'ethanol',x:'Naltrexone blocks μ receptors and reduces craving and the reward of drinking; it cannot be used in patients who take opioids. Acamprosate also reduces relapse but works on glutamate signalling and is cleared by the kidney.'},
{q:'Twenty hours after his last drink a man hears threatening voices but is fully oriented, with normal pulse and temperature. Diagnosis?',o:['Delirium tremens','Alcoholic hallucinosis','Wernicke encephalopathy','Schizophrenia'],a:1,d:'path',c:'substance-use',x:'Alcoholic hallucinosis appears 12–48 hours after the last drink with hallucinations but clear consciousness and stable vital signs. Delirium tremens comes later and brings confusion, fever and autonomic instability.'},
{q:'A man who injected heroin 2 hours ago becomes acutely sick with withdrawal minutes after his first dose of buprenorphine. Why?',o:['It is a partial agonist that displaces heroin','It is a full agonist with a short half-life','It is converted to naloxone in the liver','It blocks α₂ receptors in the locus coeruleus'],a:0,d:'pharm',c:'substance-use',x:'Buprenorphine binds μ receptors more tightly than heroin but activates them less, so it swaps a full effect for a partial one and precipitates withdrawal. That is why it is started only once withdrawal has begun; it is not converted to naloxone, though some products add naloxone to deter injection.'}
]};
